Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
 
where are you from  from sudan  have you come from cali have you come  from calais  how many of you one two three four five  17 people 12  17 14 13 of you are you okay you don't  have life jackets  you don't have jackets  we're just coming alongside you might be  able to see in the distance  the fourth boat of migrants that we have  seen  so far today and i have to say you know  it is an unsettling  image coming this close hello  you okay okay  are you okay  where are you from  we're very close you can see to um the  white cliffs of dover now  our boat um driver is just calling the  coast guard now so obviously we'll stay  with this boat make sure  it's safe as it comes into shore but it  really has been quite an extraordinary  morning out on the english channel of  course we know this is now  a very familiar scene that doesn't make  it any less  unsettling to see we have seen three  boats  already this is the fourth one the first  was in  the distance quite a way off towards  french waters and the second  um as we came back towards dave we got  really quite close there  as well and they were cheering uk as we  drove past that second book  well in a moment we're going to hear  from our europe correspondent michelle  clifford who's in calais and our  political correspondent kate mccann  who's in westminster first though let's  go to dover speak to ash nahura now get  ashna as we  we're hearing from ali there uh four  boats that she'd spotted today what  what's happening in dover what happens  when they get there  well just a few moments ago we spotted  those suspected migrants that my  colleague ali fortescue spoke to there  people from sudan kurdistan iran  um making their way to shore here at  dover they were all wearing life jackets  by this point that's because they've  been picked up by a border force vessel  and then transferred over to the jetty  here where they were met  by authorities and police coast guard as  well who are taking their details  checking their temperature of course we  are in the midst of a pandemic still so  all of those checks do need to go ahead  this only adds now to the total of more  than 700 migrants  having made their way ashore here to  dover in small boats over the last  six days and those calm conditions only  make it  um more more of a take people taking  advantage of those calm conditions  but it doesn't mean that those waters  are calm  and still they are particularly quite  treacherous out in the middle of the  channel there we saw ali there being  um sort of surrounded by waves and if  you're in that small dinghy being driven  by often a very inexperienced pilot and  that can be incredibly dangerous and as  we heard a lot of those boats are  overwhelmed with the amount of people  that are on them we saw a majority of  them in those boats and men we did  however see one  female in there that ali referenced  yesterday here in dover we saw the home  secretary pretty patel  uh seeing for herself the issues that  the border force here having to contend  with she went out to see and saw  first hand we also saw yesterday the  ministry of defense  uh deplored an raf aircraft to  oversee the crossings from the skies  it'll be interesting to see whether we  see any of that today  of course those talks which my  colleagues will talk about shortly are  going ahead in paris later  today but among all the things they're  going to be discussing there is one  thing that britain and france  can't change and that's the geography of  where i am and of where we are  in relation to france we are 20 miles  away from  calais and no walls no blockades in the  middle of the ocean millie of the  channel i should say  is going to stop that from being a bit  of a magnet for those people  ash now thank you for that let's speak  to our europe correspondent michelle  clifford who's in calais michelle on  your side  you know there's a huge focus from  british politicians about the french  doing  more uh to stop people getting on those  boats in the first place give us an idea  of what it actually is like and how that  journey starts  well uh the the way it works is that the  migrants get in touch with the smugglers  they uh  pay them a fee and then they're told to  come to the beaches in calais  hide in the dunes and we filmed  overnight a couple of days ago  and we saw six migrants hiding in the  dunes and they wait for a phone call  from the  smugglers to say whether a boat is uh  coming or not they might have to come  back  uh several nights in a row but they're  handing over  huge sums of money as much as as little  as a thousand pounds and as much as  three or four  thousand pounds so the smugglers are  making a lot of money but they have no  regard really for the safety  of the people that they're putting into  these boats and they're very often  putting double and more the number of  people that should be on these  inflatables  a couple of nights ago we were on  another beach and we saw a boat  not heading away from calais but coming  in there was no sound of an engine  because the boat  was drifting and we managed to talk to  the migrants on board that vessel they'd  actually got out  into the channel were heading for the uk  and then suddenly  they realized they'd run out of fuel the  engine stopped working and local  fishermen say this is a common  occurrence the smugglers only put enough  fuel in  uh to allow the migrants to get into  british waters not to get to  uk uh shores and of course that meant  they were drifting they were incredibly  lucky because of the direction of the  wind  and it blew them back to france and they  got back here safely but they were very  lucky  not to have been hit by a large vessel  there are ferries  and tankers and ships traveling through  this area  um all of the time there is no shortage  of migrants here who are desperate to  get  uh to the uk they're willing to hand  over the money to the  smugglers and perhaps many of them don't  realize just how dangerous it is  out in the channel because on a clear  day  from here you can actually see the uk  shoreline it seems tantalizingly close  i really don't think that many of the  migrants realize quite what the risk  they are  taking yeah michelle thank you for that  let's go to westminster now speak to our  political correspondent kate mccann kate  the immigration minister chris philp  meeting his french counterpart today  this  is a has been an ongoing issue for  several years and there's been a lot of  money  try as you spent by both the british and  the french to try and solve this problem  so past just throwing money at it what  happens today potentially  well you're right about the fact that  this has been an ongoing issue now for  a number of years and you heard from  ashna and michelle some of the reasons  why it is still a really difficult  problem to solve  not least because there are people  willing to spend vast amounts of money  there are smugglers willing to help them  and as ashana said you can't change the  geography of where the two coastlines  are  so today this meeting chris phelp is  going out there on behalf of the uk  government it's designed really to sort  of lay the groundwork for priti patel  the home secretary to go out  later on when there is hopes of a deal  between the uk and france to try and do  more  to stop people from starting to make  that journey in the first place today  expect those talks to cover things like  the french doing more to police the  beaches where people leave from as  michelle was saying  it's quite well known there are around  five or six different areas where  migrants tend to leave  specific beaches the uk government wants  to see the french do more to patrol  those areas and stop people from making  the journey in the first place  and from the uk side there is a really  strong desire from boris johnson at the  top of government  to do more to send people back to france  if it can be proven that actually they  should have claimed asylum  in france in the first place now there  are a whole myriad of really complicated  rules governing this situation  including about the areas of safe  countries where you are supposed to or  you can make an application  and whether or not you can travel to  another safe country if you want to do  that  there are also rules around sending  people back and it's a really  complicated system that the government  will have to navigate if it wants to  make changes  all of course in the context of brexit  which makes everything much  more complex edward argher the health  minister was asked earlier on today  about the prime minister's intention  to change the laws what we are seeing  are people not only taking a legal route  in but an incredibly dangerous route for  them  and their families so it is in  everyone's interest  we tackle this what i think the prime  minister was referring to there is at  the moment  the approach to this and the returns of  people who come illegally to this  country  is governed by the dublin framework that  framework  does achieve the ability to return  illegal migrants back to france but it  can also it also has very short time  scales within which that could be done  it's very inflexible  i think what he's looking at quite  rightly  is greater flexibility in our ability to  negotiate something  which allows us greater flexibility in  returning people  who have come here illegal who've been  through due process and need to be  returned back to france  now there are all kinds of issues with  returning people  back to france there are the practical  issues of doing that the uk has actually  managed to return  fewer than 10 people using that  regulation so far because it's so  complicated and difficult to do  there are also issues like migrants  deliberately trying to hide their  fingerprints for example so it can't be  proven  where they came from and of course the  moral arguments too about asylum seekers  and whether the uk should be accepting  more people all of this will make it  very difficult  for the uk and france to strike a deal  but today is the first stage in that  process  and both sides hope they can do  something to deter people from making  that treacherous journey

